From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 20 Sep 2026 10:45:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] AML: read the clock and its reference through the locked
 accessors

CDVDClock::GetClock(double&) updates m_systemAdjust without holding
m_critSection, and both of these callers run on the renderer thread while the
player thread is in the clock. The pair is read for one purpose - to see
whether the clock has been stepped - so it does not need to be atomic, only
uncorrupted.

Take the two values through the accessors that already lock: GetClock() holds
m_critSection and GetAbsoluteClock() reads only fields written in the
constructor. The cost is that the two are sampled a few nanoseconds apart
rather than together, against a step test measured in milliseconds.

Fixing it in CDVDClock would be the tidier change and would need a lock in
shared player code for a fault only Amlogic reaches; this keeps it on our side
of the line.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

--- a/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/DVDCodecs/Video/AMLGenlock.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/DVDCodecs/Video/AMLGenlock.cpp
@@ -50,8 +50,11 @@
   // A step in the clock voids it too. Ours is not one: the read below is retaken
   // after a correction, so the alignment does not see its own work and ask for
   // itself again.
-  double absolute;
-  const double clockNow = clock.GetClock(absolute);
+  // Two locked reads instead of one unlocked pair: GetClock(double&) writes
+  // m_systemAdjust outside m_critSection, and this runs on the renderer thread.
+  // The skew between them is nanoseconds; a step is milliseconds.
+  const double clockNow = clock.GetClock();
+  const double absolute = clock.GetAbsoluteClock();
   if (m_haveClock && std::abs((clockNow - m_lastClock) - (absolute - m_lastAbsolute)) >
                          CLOCK_STEPPED)
     Restart();
--- a/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/DVDCodecs/Video/AMLLatency.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/DVDCodecs/Video/AMLLatency.cpp
@@ -552,8 +552,11 @@
   {
     // pts_video names the frame that appears at the next vblank, so the one on
     // screen is a refresh older and its timestamp fell due a period sooner.
-    double absolute;
-    const double clockNow = clock.GetClock(absolute);
+    // Two locked reads instead of one unlocked pair: GetClock(double&) writes
+    // m_systemAdjust outside m_critSection, and this runs on the renderer
+    // thread. The skew between them is nanoseconds; a step is milliseconds.
+    const double clockNow = clock.GetClock();
+    const double absolute = clock.GetAbsoluteClock();
 
     // Both ends advance by about a frame between presented frames, so what is
     // left over is somebody moving the clock. Measured against the frame
